From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: meanaverage <18336095+meanaverage@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 17:17:35 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(graphics,egfx): allocate the ClearCodec image fallibly
 and convert it to RGBA in place

The ClearCodec decoder allocated its output, width * height * 4 bytes,
with vec! before reading the stream, and the EGFX client then copied
the decoded image into a second buffer of the same size to swap its
blue and red bytes. The destination rectangle that sizes it is the
server's: the decoder caps each side at 8192 pixels, and the client
only warns when the rectangle is larger than its surface. A server
could send a 41-byte ClearCodec command, a stream without layers for an
8192x8192 rectangle on any surface, and make the client hold 512 MiB
for it, allocated infallibly. In the WebAssembly build, the command
grew the memory of the instance every session shares by 513 MiB, and
when the instance was near its 4 GiB, it trapped rather than failing
the session. A server could send one with every PDU.

The decoder now reserves its output with try_reserve_exact, as the
Planar decoder does, and fails with a decode error when there is not
the memory for it, and the EGFX client swaps the bytes of the decoded
image in place. That command now takes 256 MiB, once, and near 4 GiB
it fails the session instead of trapping. A ClearCodec image still
takes up to 256 MiB, 8192x8192 pixels at four bytes: it cannot be sized
from the stream, since a residual run of a few bytes fills all of it,
and 8192 is the per-axis cap the decoder already had, room for an 8K
monitor.

The allocator of the peak_heap test binary can now make allocations
past a limit fail, as when memory runs out. With it, a ClearCodec
command and a Planar command (whose buffers an earlier commit of this
series made fallible) that do not fit in memory fail with an error
rather than aborting the tests.
